n this case they were  advertisers  and we ran the experiment for a short  period of time to make sure  nothing was totally borked and it seemed  like our revenue was okay  and so we increased the size of the  experiment and  uh in week two we launched this new  experiment with even more segments  then we decided some of these conditions  don't really work well it doesn't really  tell us  information about trade-offs that we're  trying to explore with the experiment  so we changed the number of experimental  conditions  added a few more conditions and so now  this is an entirely different plan out  script  and then we launched it to many users  and analyzed that over a long period of  time  and then eventually made a launch  decision  yeah a segment is just a partitioning of  the primary so the question was what do  i mean by segments  a segment is just a partitioning of the  primary units so if you're primarily  experimenting with users  uh users would belong to segments and so  you have some number of segments let's  say 10  000 and each of those segments is  randomly assigned  to an experiment and once that's  assigned to the experiment  there's additional random assignment  that occurs within that experiment  so once we go through the code example  hopefully it'll be a little bit  clearer how this works i know it's a  little abstract  so we have this class called simple  namespace  that we'll be using and then also  another class called default experiment  which is a lot like simple experiment  but it doesn't do any logging  and also has some functionality for just  storing dictionaries of values  so in general  namespaces represent a set of related  parameters  so things that have to do with uh all of  them  are like the ranking model for search or  all of them have to do  with the display of product results in  your search feed  or have to do with things that relate to  pricing  of certain goods and all of those  experiments are necessarily mutually  exclusive so you don't have to use a  namespace to manage experiments  that aren't actually modifying the same  variable that's used  in your code right  in general you should never modify an  already running experiment  you should instead launch new  experiments through namespaces  and experiments should be analyzed  separately  unless you really know what you're doing  and are using statistical procedures  that are designed to pool across related  experiments  so let's say that we have multiple  versions of an experiment  in this case we have  an experiment that has to do with a  banner that  is at the top of the user screen and the  first experiment it  says hello there or welcome uh  second version it says hello there  welcome with different probabilities  so same parameters different assignment  probabilities  third version uh it says  completely different things so these are  three versions of an experiment  they're all setting the same related  parameter which is the banner  and then we have this concept of a  default experiment so if the user  doesn't  belong to any experiment what is the  parameter  that we're going to return if it's  requested so in this case it's just  a generic generic greetings is the text  now here's how we define namespaces with  button namespace  uh this simple namespace is really just  a code example  that i'm providing for people to  understand how namespaces work  in general you would set up namespaces  through some kind of graphical user  interface that's available to engineers  on your website and stored via database  so for this particular class what we  have to do is specify the name  of your namespace which gets used to  salt  your hashing procedures you have to  specify a primary unit  so this is the unit that gets used uh to  assign inputs to segments  so you could potentially have an  experiment in a namespace  that operates off of user  segment pairs but it would partition it  initially by the user id  and then you specify some number of  segments so for the demonstration  version  i'm saying 100 in a production  environment you would want to use  something like 10 000 segments or 100  000 segments  and then when users are setting up the  experiments uh  they shouldn't really be aware of the  fact that like users or experiments are  allocated in terms  of segment numbers but in terms of  percentages  and then you can specify a default  experiment class so if a user  or an input does not belong to an  experiment this value is instead of  returned  and then there's this kind of stub  function which kind of  simulates what would happen if we were  to add or remove  experiments from the namespace again  this is something that would be done  via a database or a graphical user  interface in a production environment  so what was what we're saying here is  that we've got  three different experiments the first  two experiments  use the same uh planout script or  assignment procedure  and the first one has 30 segments the  second one has 10 segments  and the last one has 40 segments  and they all have different experiment  names so if we try this out with a few  users  here we see hello there  generic greetings  etc we could run this for a bunch of  input units  you can see that these are are clearly  in some cases coming from the default  experiment in other cases they're coming  from  v1 and other cases they're coming from  v2  and so what's going on underneath the  hood well we're giving it  let's say we give it user id 2  user id 2 belongs to segment number 2029  and if we look at the the segment  allocations  we can see how each segment number is  mapped to a particular  experiment name and here you see that 29  doesn't exist here  so this particular segment will not be  assigned to an experiment and instead  uses the default value store  and so when we do this this is going to  return  null and so we see that  the individual unit is not allocated to  the experiment  so if we were to try this with a  different user id you know  sometimes it'll i guess most segments  are not actually allocated to  conditions  but we can see that the number of  segments that we have  or experiments that are running do  correspond to the number of segments  that were  initially allocated  okay well that concludes  uh the tutorial for the most part  um again i would highly suggest using  namespaces if you're running  multiple experiments and to centrally  manage your experiments  and to analyze your experiments with  care if you want to somehow pool across  experiments or  run follow-on experiments  so just to recap uh i believe that  experiments are really useful for  knowledge building they're not just  useful  for doing av tests to optimize your site  there's a lot you can learn about your  users and your products  through running more sophisticated  experiments uh and that this plan out  system that we've developed supports  good experimentation practices  and focuses people on parameters and  makes it easy to do more complex things  rather than  baking off two alternatives  and if you use our system or adopt some  of the ideas from our system  it'll prevent you from shooting yourself  in the foot  and oh uh i should think  you can download it i guess hopefully a  lot of you have done that already  my collaborators on this project are  dean echols and michael bernstein  and i'd also like to thank a number of  engineers who worked on the  experimentation system  that a lot of the system is modeled off  of
 

